posts.body:
Hi, all!
I'm having an issue in trying to develop an idea for my game which would require some custom parameters to rise/fall over the course of gameplay. VisuStella gives support with adding them easily enough, but I'm lost as to how to go about targeting each one, specifically, in an event to increase or decrease it's value.
I'd like to use their plugin because the stats are reflected in the status screen, (vs just using variables) so any help with this would be appreciated! The best I've come up with is;
JavaScript:
$gameActors.actor(1)._CustomParam['wisdom'] += 10;
$gameActors.actor(1).refresh();
But it does nothing. (◞‸◟,)

Hey, there! Thanks for taking the time to reply! I see, so VS doesn't have the logic necessary to have it's parameters called on. I'm using the VS core engine method yes! I haven't touched the default JS so it looks like this;
JavaScript:
// Declare Constants
const user = this;

// Calculations
return (user.agi * 0.75) + (user.atk * 0.25);
I put your edit into the strength stat's JS field, and it returns 0, which is great! I just don't know how to increase it from there via events/scripting. I thought there was a way to have a script reference the custom stat and then allow me to adjust it? But like you said, that doesn't seem to be the case... (And even if it was, I'm not sure how I would separate the values among the party members? (So that all their numbers aren't the same.))
Eli's plugin is a dream! But I don't think it's custom stats are reflected in any menus? If it had a VS status window patch, that would be perfect!
But most likely, I'll need to re-think this whole idea. ^A^;;

Allusion said:
Hi, thank you so much for your reply! Yes, the custom stats can be referenced with their abbreviation or their full name, according to the instructions. Setting the value in the plugin parameters is definitely where I started, but I'm having trouble adjusting them via events. (I'm trying to make it so that the custom stats can increase or decrease based on the player's choices/actions! Vs leveling up or so. I just don't know if that's even possible...)
Click to expand...
I'm a bit confused what's the problem?
If your return formula is dependent on user.atk (like in the example), then you change the attack value in the events to change the custom parameter. If your return value is dependent on variables, then you change those variables.
EDIT:
If you want different variables for your actors, it's (worked for me at least):
if (user.actorId() == 1) {
return $gameVariables.value(99);
} else if (user.actorId() == 2) {
return $gameVariables.value(100);
}

